    {
      "question": "e-13138",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "506821a93347db1debe382dcc59fd1f1bf6a506670dc1f9a4ab8727a22f9e2e5",
      "new_sha256": "1316531b52108c2f6213b84dfebbbdfa58c4cb22073860ba3d8979d89fb55a5a",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-13138",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/assets/67ef97be4b21a74feaae15f3ff30d5077fc1316874df8de79155316a621e4487.png)\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$ represents the set of **natural numbers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$ represents the set of **integers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb{Q}$ represents the set of **rational numbers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}$ represents the set of **real numbers**.\n\n- $\\mathbb C$ represents the set of **complex numbers**.\n\nWith that in mind, we note the following: \n\n- The number $3.25$ is not an integer, so it does *not* belong to $\\mathbb{Z}$ nor $\\mathbb{N},$  so we can write\n\n$$\n3.25\\notin\\mathbb N, \\qquad 3.25\\notin\\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\n- However, notice that $3.25$ can be written as the ratio of two integers as follows:\n\n$$\n3.25=\\dfrac{325}{100}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $3.25$ is a rational number, and we can write\n\n$$\n3.25 \\in \\mathbb{Q}.\n$$\n\n- Every rational is a real and complex number. Consequently, $3.25$ belongs to both $\\mathbb R$ and $\\mathbb{C},$ and we can write\n\n$$\n3.25 \\in \\mathbb{R}, \\qquad 3.25 \\in \\mathbb{C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbb Q, \\mathbb R,$ and $\\mathbb C$ only.\"",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035971/example-13138.json",
          "file_sha256": "19200912422a2262af1032a986df26de1fd71e0ec26215628b460b7bebf67ed3"
        }
      ],
      "basis": 1332,
